.\" -*- mode: nroff; -*-
.Dd August 24, 2025
.Dt NOCT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noct
.Nd Noct programming language CLI
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar file
.br
.Nm
.Op Fl Fl gpu Ns Op Cm = Ns Ar selector
.Ar source-file
.br
.Nm
.Fl Fl gpu-list
.br
.Nm
.Fl Fl compile
.Ar files ...
.br
.Nm
.Fl Fl ansic
.Ar out.c
.Ar in files ...
.br
.Nm
.Fl Fl elisp
.Ar out.el
.Ar in files ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command is the command-line interface for the Noct programming language.
It can directly run a program, run REPL, compile sources into Noct bytecode,
or transpile sources to ANSI C or Emacs Lisp.
.Pp
If no arguments are provided, runs the REPL.
.Pp
If no option is given and a
.Ar file
is provided,
.Nm
executes the program.
.Pp
The
.Sy __accel
function annotation is an optimization hint.
Its checked CPU body remains valid and runs when accelerator optimization is
unavailable or declined.
Accelerator-enabled builds choose platform backends automatically: Direct3D
12 on Windows, Vulkan and OpenGL ES on Linux and
.Fx ,
and Metal on macOS.
The compiler uses a private, backend-specific
.Sy __Accel
package; there is no public
.Sy Accel.*
API.
.Pp
CPU code may divide eligible loops into multiple synchronous GPU regions in
one function.
This means successive offload sessions on the selected device, not
simultaneous use of multiple physical GPUs.
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width 14n
.It Fl Fl compile
Compile one or more input source files into Noct bytecode files.
.It Fl Fl gpu Ns Op Cm = Ns Ar selector
Enable accelerator optimization while running source.
Without a selector, choose a default suitable device.
Canonical selectors, as printed by
.Fl Fl gpu-list ,
begin with
.Ql vulkan\&: ,
.Ql opengles\&: ,
.Ql d3d12\&: ,
or
.Ql metal\&: .
An exact unqualified device name is accepted only when it is unique across
the available backends.
.Pp
This option applies only to source execution, including source supplied with
.Fl e .
It cannot be used with a
.Pa .nbc
or
.Pa .nap
file,
.Fl Fl compile ,
or
.Fl Fl app .
.It Fl Fl gpu-list
List suitable GPU devices and their canonical selectors, then exit.
This option must be used by itself.
.It Fl Fl ansic Ar out.c Ar in files ...
Transpile the specified input source files into a single ANSI C source file
.Ar out.c .
The generated C file can be compiled with the Noct runtime.
.It Fl Fl elisp Ar out.el Ar in files ...
Transpile the specified input source files into a single Emacs Lisp file
.Ar out.el .
The resulting file can be loaded into Emacs to run the program using
Noct's Emacs integration (if available).
.El
.Sh OPERANDS
.Bl -tag -width 14n
.It Ar file
Path to a Noct source file to execute.
When present without other options,
.Nm
runs the program.
.It Ar files
One or more input source files.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Nm
exits 0 on success and >0 if an error occurs (syntax error, I/O failure,
code generation error, or runtime failure).
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run REPL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noct
.Ed
.Pp
Run a program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noct hello.noct
.Ed
.Pp
Compile multiple sources to bytecode:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noct --compile lib.noct main.noct
.Ed
.Pp
Transpile to ANSI C:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noct --ansic aot_source.c main.noct lib.noct
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a single Emacs Lisp file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noct --elisp app.el main.noct lib.noct
.Ed
.Sh VERSION
Noct Programming Language
.Dv 0.1.0
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Awe Morris
and the Noct contributors.
.Sh BUGS
Report issues at <awe@noctvm.io> .
.Pp
Behavior and options are subject to change while Noct is pre-1.0.
